  "id": "zulu_ingoma_dance",
  "name": "Ingoma (Zulu competitive dance-song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Zulu dance-song genre, also called indlamu, performed by teams of young men, rooted in rural KwaZulu-Natal and developed by migrant workers in Durban and Witwatersrand hostels into weekend competitions, with regional styles such as isishameni and isizingili. Dancers sing in call and response and end phrases with high kicks and forceful stamps that land together, often with drums and clapping. Hugh Tracey recorded ingoma for ILAM, and Louise Meintjes studied ngoma teams in Dust of the Zulu. The membrane-drum card stands in for the drum.",
  "recipe": "classic modern ingoma, groove Percussion, central east south African branch. single leader group response. foot stomp, hand clap, membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
